Z||000000||DreXteR||08-15-2001||08:54 PM||||Soon I will start the rebuild process of the GT engine. But have some doubts on a sort of things to replace.<P>1. The cam - Since my project will envolve a turbo in near future the rebuild will be based to that. I dont know what to do, buy a CompuCam 2030 or buy the GM Roller cam(if works in the block) or keep the used. Also the posibility of a set of roller rockers 1.6.<P>2. There is performace diference between the dif ignition modules?  As far I know fierostore sell eaton, also Accel and Holley makes them.<P>3. SB Rods can be used just has to be .080 narrowed. But I find dif lengths, wich one will do the trick????<P>4. What is the mod in the cranck area for better oiling???<P>Thanx<P>DreX<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://users.centennialpr.net/drexter/Fiero/Fiero1.jpg">  <BR>86 GT Turbo Intercooler Project - Total Restoration.||||reg||1
Z||000001||DreXteR||08-15-2001||08:59 PM||||WWWOOOOoooooOOOOOOOOOOOOooooooooOOOOOooooOO!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!<P><BR>200 POst!<P>Err...  201 with this   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000002||fix||08-15-2001||09:21 PM||||just my opinion, but sounds like alot of work for a 2.8.  I don't know a whole lot about this, but I recall hearing from more than one person that they regret rebuilding their 2.8's because they could have swapped a 3.1 or 3.4 in for about the same price.  not sure, but that's what I heard.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||08-15-2001||09:34 PM||||If you want to keep it stock (with the 2.8) trash the cam and get a new one.<BR>The replacement cam depends on how your going to set up the turbo. For instance how much boost are you going to run? Is your car a stick or auto?<BR>You won't go wrong with the compucam either way. If you want to go with the roller you should look into a steel crank, especially if you have a stick shift.<BR>What's up with the rods? Different lengths? NO way can you have different lengths!<BR>If you want to go with a 3.1 conversion as a rebuild you need a crank, rods and pistons from a 3.1 if I'm not mistaken.<BR>Stick with A/C Delco module, the rest are prone to early failure from comments made here.<BR>Small block rods, I have no idea!<BR>?4? . . . no help on that one.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DreXteR||08-15-2001||10:26 PM||||Well, I will stick to 2.8 because I have a set of forged pistons 8.1:1 + are .030 over. About car specs is a 4speed stick. Also the plan is 6~10psi for normal use and 18psi for race.<P>By the lengths I refeer that there is a dif lengths of sb rods and need to know wich one will work with 2.8. <P>Thanx<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||08-16-2001||12:56 AM||||You need to get the early SBC rods (2 in main journals) and have them narrowed (.086 in) and new bearing tang slots machined in.  The stock length is 5.7 inches and matches the 2.8 exactly.  This is usually only done when using a racing rod as the SBCs are only slightly stronger than the original 2.8 in strength.  As for the crank, if you plan on 10 & 11 grand revs with 12:1 pistons then yeah put a forged steel crank in it (expensive), otherwise the stock cranks are very stout due to their short length and journal design and good for abuse at 7 grand.  You want to have it checked for cracks and grind any excess flashing off of it.  If you are going to build a total strip or track eng then yes you should crossdrill the center two main journals to give more oil at high RPM.   Pick-up a copy of "Chevrolet Power" from HPBooks at a bookstore.  GL<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 08-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||DreXteR||08-16-2001||02:45 AM||||For only slightly stronger I believe keep mines or try to get forged or racing sb rods.<P>thanx for the imput.<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000007||Joe Torma||08-16-2001||10:26 AM||||Stock 2.8 block do not have threaded holes for the roller lifters guide block(an interesting piece of plastic), so you'd have to drill and tread holes, machine the block, and hope you have them right.  The small block rods is a "recommended" thing for all 2.8-3.4 engines, but like GTZ says everything is fine till 7000.  I don't like Compucams, just my opinion.  Cross drilling the crank is ok if you are going to endurance race the thing.  Just a waste for daily driving/weekend racing.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 08-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||DreXteR||08-16-2001||10:37 AM||||Ok, by now the num 2, 3 and 4(almost) has been clarified. But the cam issue still in the air. Thanx for sharing knowledge and opinion. Still need more info for the cam situation.<P>Thanx again<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000009||Capt Fiero||08-16-2001||11:19 AM||||The stock cam just plain sucks.  And if it has been in there for a while, I will almost bet money that at least one of the lobes is showing its wear and is not giving you as much lift and duration as it should.  I would recomend a cam with around .420 lift.  If you are planning on forced induction, you might want to go a bit bigger on the exhaust lobe say .430 or so.  I am sure that there has to be at least one turbo guy on the list.  I have the cam specs posted on my new web page.  With the 1.52 roller tip rockes, I have a gross lift of .426 intake and exhaust.  Stock is .393 and .410.  But my duration is much more than stock at 280'  (208@.050)  I hope this link works, so take a browse around I think you will find at least some of the info usefull.  <BR> <A HREF="http://members.shaw.ca/hotrodfiero/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.shaw.ca/hotrodfiero/index.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Joe Torma||08-16-2001||11:37 AM||||I don't like the mass produced cams.  Sure it will work with the computer, but what about with your new chip(no flames please), different injectors, or whatever.<P>I would call up a custom cam shop and ask them to grind a custom one for you that will work with what your trying to do.  They have thousands of grind profiles on file.  I used DeltaCam.  I am very happy with my cam.<P>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000011||DreXteR||08-16-2001||01:43 PM||||Joe - Thanx  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Also chech for a roller version.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Also consider the posibility of 1.6 or 1.5 roller rockers. Too much lift could end in some problems.<P>BTW, some1 have the specs of the roller cam from GM???<P>Thanx<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000012||Neal||08-16-2001||01:48 PM||||The compcam 2030 is great for normal driving, no huge loss of lowend TQ.  But joe is right if your after somthing special get a custom grind on your old cam, may even be cheaper than a new cam.<P>with a turbo and low revs a compucam will be fine.  To much cam and youll have trouble making boost at low revs.<P>And if your going to narrow rods, dont get forged.  Forged is good because it keeps all of the origional metal grains intact and by narrowing the rods your destroying origional grains, the reason for forging...||||reg||1
Z||000013||87GTZ34||08-16-2001||02:15 PM||||Neal, narrowing a forged rod doesn't hurt the grain.  The rods have to be machined when they are produced anyway as they don't 'forge' to specs, they forge a piece of raw metal and machine it to specs.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Wannabe||08-16-2001||03:20 PM||||Turbos like lots of lift with short to moderate duration and minimal overlap. For most purposes other than strictly racing, you don't need to build for high RPMs. A turbo'd 2.8 will make LOTS of power before 6000 rpm. Fiero Shop in Toronto has a turbo'd 2.8 that has put out around 280 RWHP and run consistent mid to low 13's on the stock bottom end. Chevy rods are not a bad idea, but a forged crank may be overkill. Definitely do what you can to improve oiling.||||reg||1
Z||000015||DreXteR||08-16-2001||03:55 PM||||Wannabe - What are the specs of the 2.8 turbo from Fiero Shop??<P>So, its good to make the oil mod?<P>Thanx<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000016||Joe Torma||08-16-2001||05:00 PM||||Eh, I say just get a high volume oil pump and use synthetic if you worry.<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000017||mrfixit58||08-16-2001||05:02 PM||||I agree with Mr. Fix. If your point is more power, buy a 3.4 short block to build. You'll gain 20+ hp right from the start. You can probably get one in need of rebuild cheap. The dollars to build the 2.8 or 3.4 are the same. And you may find a better cam selection (reaching here guys) for the 3.4 because they were put into Camaros and Firebirds.<P>As far as the cam design, why not pick up the telephone and call Crane. Tell them what you want to build and see what they recommend. These guys do cams for a living. It would be hard for me to believe they would steer you wrong.<P>Keep us posted,<P>------------------<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>87 GT    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by mrfixit58 (edited 08-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||DreXteR||08-17-2001||11:11 AM||||       <P><BR>       |<BR>       ^<BR>      / \<BR>ToP   ||<BR>      | |<BR>     /___\<BR>     |*|*|<BR>      | |<BR>       | ||207.227.82.141||reg||1
Z||000019||Wannabe||08-18-2001||09:17 PM||||You'll need to contact the Fiero Shop to get more details on their install. I do know that they used the FOCOA headers with ceramic coating, a custom chip and an msd ignition. They were running about 10-12 lbs boost and an intercooler from an Eagle Talon when they got their best numbers. The bottom end finally died due to to a fuel delivery problem, but this was after somewhere around 100 runs in the 13's. Only one piston was burned when it happened. I *think* they are working on offering this set up as a kit, or at least have considered it. Just to give an idea the importance of the camshaft, after the 2.8 went, they built a 3.1 with a stronger bottom end and some addtional prep. The 3.1 made substantially less power than the 2.8 because the engine builder installed a higher duration cam with too much overlap for the turbo.<p>[This message has been edited by Wannabe (edited 08-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||DreXteR||08-18-2001||11:43 PM||||Wannabe - Regarding to the cam, If I use 1.6 rocker arms is best keep the stock cam?!<P>Or use a 1.5 rocker arm with a high lift cam(is the same) .<P>DreX||||reg||1
